{
  "id": "2026-10-18-estonia-voting-code",
  "title": "Estonia lets anyone read the code behind its online elections",
  "topic": "테크",
  "level": 2,
  "voice": "en-US-AndrewMultilingualNeural",
  "sentences": [
    "Good morning!",
    "Imagine voting in a national election from your couch, using your laptop.",
    "In Estonia, that has been normal for about twenty years.",
    "This week, a lot of developers were talking about the source code of that system.",
    "It sits in a public repository on GitHub, where anyone can read it.",
    "The election office says the repository isn't used for development.",
    "Instead, it's kept in sync with the exact code that runs in real elections.",
    "The idea is simple: if people can inspect the code, they're more likely to trust the result.",
    "The system is split into many small parts, such as vote collection, storage, and counting.",
    "There's even a separate app that lets voters verify their vote was recorded correctly.",
    "Still, publishing code doesn't automatically make it safe.",
    "Researchers point out that very few experts have actually reviewed it line by line.",
    "In other words, open code only helps if someone takes the time to read it.",
    "So here's a question for your next coffee break.",
    "Would you trust an online vote more if you could read the code yourself?"
  ],
  "summary_ko": [
    "에스토니아는 약 20년째 온라인으로 국정 선거 투표를 한다. 그 시스템(IVXV)의 소스 코드가 GitHub 에 공개돼 있어 이번 주 개발자들 사이에서 화제가 됐다.",
    "선거관리청은 이 저장소에서 개발하지 않고, 실제 선거에 쓰는 코드와 똑같이 맞춰둔다고 한다. 누구나 검토할 수 있어야 결과를 믿을 수 있다는 취지다.",
    "다만 연구자들은 공개된 지 오래됐는데도 코드를 한 줄씩 실제로 검토한 전문가는 드물다고 지적한다 — 공개만으로 안전해지지는 않는다."
  ],
